{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794187378,"nonce":"iq8i8D7QY10YbuqwoNWDNbZs3/g=","message":"Owner decision 2026-11-09: build the cross-module import convention before pipeline-constant variants (5976a11); variants then become a use-site choice.\n\nConvention, proven in a scratch workspace (same crate by a relative path, across crates, and a dependency reached through a re-exported alias):\n- Each russl! expansion defines `#[macro_export] macro_rules! __russl_source_\u003cid\u003e` (id: hash of the source tokens and call site) and `#[doc(hidden)] pub use __russl_source_\u003cid\u003e as __russl_source;` in its Rust module. Invoked as `P::__russl_source! { ::russl::__russl_continue { state } }`, it appends `module { id, deps, imports, source }` to the state and calls the continuation.\n- A module's own compile loads its whole import graph, so at the end it re-exports every transitive dependency flat: `#[doc(hidden)] pub use \u003chow it reached it\u003e as __russl_dep_\u003ck\u003e;`, listing their ids in its source macro. An importer reaches a dependency's dependencies as `P::__russl_dep_\u003ck\u003e`, never through a path chain, and skips ids it already holds (diamonds load once).\n- The source macro also carries the module's import map: each `use` path as written, and the module id it resolved to, so the compiler resolves an imported module's own `use` items without knowing where it lives.\n- One russl module per Rust module (two would define `__russl_source` twice). Cycles cannot expand: each source macro exists only once its module's chain has finished.\n\nCompiler: per-module scopes; only `pub` items importable, `pub use` re-exports, `as` aliases, globs; only the root's entry points are lowered, and only what they reach (functions, constants, globals); a module with no entry points is a library: checked, no artifacts. Warnings for imported modules come from their own compile, not again from each importer.","files":null}]}